WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.230
Kelly, see Pete and I live in the same building.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.150
So we decided to meet at 8 a.m.

3
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.030
8 a.m. to come to work together.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.550
I had waited 15 minutes when Pete arrived.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.730
After Pete had wasted time waiting for coffee, we were late.

6
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.510
I left you a message.

7
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.610
I didn't get that message.

8
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.730
Oh, sorry.

9
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:33.190
Pete?

10
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.030
Anna left a message.

11
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.470
That's the right thing to do.

12
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.730
Did you find some examples of the past perfect tense?

13
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:43.590
I did.

14
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.650
Anna said.

15
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.730
After Pete had wasted time waiting for coffee, we were late.

16
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.610
Look at that coffee.

17
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.610
It looks more like dessert.

18
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:54.570
Okay.

19
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.670
Deep watching for past perfect.

20
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.570
Yeah, that's not why we're late.

21
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.510
This is why we're late.

22
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.130
I had arrived on time at 8 a.m.

23
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.990
but didn't see Anna.

24
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.270
She was standing behind a tree.

25
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.790
I think she was hugging it.

26
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.930
I always walk to work,

27
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.630
but she said that would take too long

28
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.150
and that a scooter would be faster.

29
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.190
It was awful.

30
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.390
I hated it and it added too much time to our commute.

31
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.750
Then Anna stopped by a pond to feed the birds.

32
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.450
She had named them after characters from books

33
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.690
and yelled the names out loud.

34
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.870
Romeo, Juliet, Sherlock.

35
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.150
By the time she had fed all the birds, we were late.

36
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.430
This is what I think.

37
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.690
You two see the same event very differently.

38
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.150
Does this happen often with you two?

39
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:00.290
Yes.

40
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:00.510
No.

41
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:01.910
Yes.

42
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.490
What are you talking about?

43
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:04.990
Okay.

44
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.130
This is good.

45
00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.010
This is good.

46
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.150
It's good to see things differently.

47
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.770
I have an idea.

48
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.950
We will call the show

49
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.690
He Said, She Said.

50
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.210
For every story, you tell a different point of view.

51
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.690
That is a great idea, Kelly.

52
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.950
Pete, we are different.

53
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.830
That's why I thought of you for this job.

54
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.310
I think you two understand perfectly.

55
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.650
Let's get to work.
